! The spin-spiral twin of h-fcc-magnon.in -- the same crystal, the same grid,
! the same functional -- run through the generalized Bloch theorem instead.
!
! **It exists to reproduce a negative result, not a comparison.** The plan was
! the obvious pairing: the transverse susceptibility says the ferromagnet is
! linearly unstable wherever the leading eigenvalue of X_0 F exceeds one, and a
! scan of E(q) over this input should say whether a spiral at that q is actually
! lower in energy -- two calculations sharing no machinery at all, one a sum
! over states plus a matrix inversion on a fixed density, the other a sequence
! of independent self-consistent fields.
!
! It does not work, and what it does instead is worth being able to reproduce:
! **the spiral's own SCF leaves the magnetic branch.** At q = (0, 0, 1/2) the
! 90-degree spiral converges to |m| = 0.0001 -- the nonmagnetic solution -- so
! its 59 meV "gain" is demagnetization and not a spin-wave energy at all; at
! q = (0, 0, 1/4) it lands on a different collinear solution at |m| = 0.248.
! Starting from a 15-degree cone does not help: the cone collapses to zero at
! 1/4 and the moment to zero at 1/2, because nothing holds either. Elk's own
! Ni-magnon-spiral example runs fsmtype = -1 with a large field along momfix
! for exactly that reason.
!
! PLAN.md P63 records the measurement and what replaced it in the tests.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 6.5,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.01,
    noncolin = .true.
    nosym = .true., noinv = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 1.0
    angle1(1) = 90.0
    spiral_q(1) = 0.0, spiral_q(2) = 0.0, spiral_q(3) = 0.0
    nbnd = 8
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 4 4 4 0 0 0
